{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Zone — declarative rules{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Zone — declarative access rules</h1>
    <p>
        This whole area is guarded from the outside by
        <code>access_control.rules</code>. <code>ZoneController</code> carries
        <strong>no <code>#[AccessPolicy]</code> attribute at all</strong> — access is
        decided on <code>kernel.request</code>, before any controller runs. It is the
        config-driven counterpart to the attribute-based pages elsewhere in this demo, and
        the options are exactly those of <code>security.access_control</code>.
    </p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Page</th><th>Rule</th><th>Grants</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_members') }}">/zone/members</a></td>
                <td><code>roles: [ROLE_USER]</code></td>
                <td>any signed-in account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_staff') }}">/zone/staff</a></td>
                <td><code>roles: [ROLE_MANAGER]</code></td>
                <td>manager, admin (via hierarchy)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_admin') }}">/zone/admin</a></td>
                <td><code>roles: [ROLE_ADMIN]</code></td>
                <td>admin</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_local') }}">/zone/local</a></td>
                <td><code>allow_if: request.getClientIp() in [...]</code></td>
                <td>anyone on localhost — no role</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_api') }}">/zone/api</a></td>
                <td><code>methods: [POST], roles: [ROLE_MANAGER]</code></td>
                <td>GET open to all; POST needs a manager</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="{{ path('app_zone_secure') }}">/zone/secure</a></td>
                <td><code>requires_channel: https, roles: [ROLE_USER]</code></td>
                <td>redirected to HTTPS first, then any user</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="muted">
        <code>requires_channel</code> is the odd one out: it is about the transport and not about
        who is asking, so it corrects rather than refuses. The component's channel listener answers
        it ahead of the rule listener, and the firewall's own is taken out of the chain — one rule,
        one redirect.
    </p>

    <p class="muted">
        A denial here throws the component's <code>AccessDeniedException</code>. The firewall
        catches it through the shared <code>AccessDeniedExceptionInterface</code>: an anonymous
        visitor is redirected to the login page, a signed-in but forbidden one gets a 403.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
